@node Naming system
@section Naming System

Smalltalk uses @code{case-sensitive} naming with identifiers normalized
to @code{camelCase}. The lexer converts PascalCase and snake_case
identifiers to camelCase format.

@example
myVariable   @result{} myVariable
playerHealth @result{} playerHealth
SPRITE_COUNT  @result{} spriteCount
@end example

@table @asis
@item Keywords / Messages
@code{ifTrue:}, @code{ifFalse:}, @code{ifTrue:ifFalse:},
@code{whileTrue:}, @code{whileFalse:}, @code{to:do:},
@code{timesRepeat:}, @code{do:}, @code{new}, @code{self}, @code{super},
@code{true}, @code{false}, @code{nil}, @code{and:}, @code{or:},
@code{dialogue:}, @code{print}, @code{display}, @code{input}, @code{read},
@code{say:}, @code{ask:}, @code{copy:}

@item Special Variables
@code{self} — reference to the current object
@code{super} — reference for method lookup in superclass
@code{thisContext} — current execution context (advanced)
@code{nil} — undefined object (false/nothing)
@code{true} / @code{false} — Boolean objects

@item Operators
@code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, @code{/}, @code{//} (integer division),
@code{\\} (modulo), @code{=}, @code{~=}, @code{==}, @code{>}, @code{<},
@code{>=}, @code{<=}, @code{,} (sequence)

@item String Delimiters
Single quotes (@code{'...'}) for strings
Double quotes (@code{"..."}) for comments

@item Symbol Delimiters
Hash prefix (@code{#name}) for symbols

@item Comments
Double quotes to end of line (@code{" comment "})
@end table

@node Classes and objects
@section Classes and Objects

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Smalltalk is a purely object-oriented language where everything is an
object. Classes define the structure and behavior of objects; instances
are created from classes.

@subsubheading Class Definition

@example
Object subclass: #@var{ClassName}
    instanceVariableNames: '@var{inst1} @var{inst2} ...'
    classVariableNames: '@var{class1} @var{class2} ...'
    poolDictionaries: ''
    category: '@var{category}'
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
Object subclass: #Player
    instanceVariableNames: 'x y health sprite'
    classVariableNames: 'MaxPlayers'
    poolDictionaries: ''
    category: 'Game-Entities'
@end example

@subsubheading Creating Instances

@example
@var{instance} := @var{Class} new.
@var{instance} := @var{Class} new.
@var{instance} initialize.
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
player := Player new.
player initialize.
enemy := Enemy new.
@end example

@subsubheading Instance Methods

@example
@var{ClassName>>@var{methodName}
    @var{statements}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
Player>>initialize
    x := 0.
    y := 0.
    health := 100.
    sprite := 0.

Player>>moveDX: dx dy: dy
    x := x + dx.
    y := y + dy.

Player>>isAlive
    ^ health > 0
@end example

@subsubheading Class Methods

@example
@var{ClassName class>>@var{methodName}
    @var{statements}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
Player class>>newWithX: xValue y: yValue
    | p |
    p := self new.
    p x: xValue; y: yValue.
    ^ p
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Class names: UpperCamelCase, globally unique
@item Instance variable names: lowercase, space-separated
@item Class variable names: PascalCase, shared across instances
@end itemize

@node Variables and declarations
@section Variables and Declarations

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Local variables hold temporary values within a method. They must be
declared in a pipe-delimited list before use.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
| @var{var1} @var{var2} @var{var3} |
@var{statements}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
| score lives |
score := 0.
lives := 3.

| x y z temp |
x := 10.
y := 20.
temp := x.
x := y.
y := temp.

| name age |
name := 'Player'.
age := 25.
@end example

@subsubheading Temporary Variables

Variables declared at method level are temporary and scoped to that
method execution.

@subsubheading Instance Variables

Accessed directly within instance methods:

@example
Player>>setPositionX: xValue y: yValue
    x := xValue.
    y := yValue.
@end example

@subsubheading Global Variables (Class Variables)

Shared across all instances of a class and its subclasses.

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Variable names: lowercase letters, digits, underscores
@item Declaration: must precede any statement in a sequence
@item Scope: block or method where declared
@end itemize

@node Blocks (BlockClosure)
@section Blocks (BlockClosure)

@findex [
@findex ]

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Blocks are anonymous functions (closures) that can be stored in
variables, passed as arguments, and executed later. They capture their
lexical environment.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
[ @var{statements} ]
[ :@var{param1} :@var{param2} | @var{statements} ]
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Simple block
[ Transcript show: 'Hello' ]

-- Block with value
[ x + y ]

-- Block with parameter
[ :value | value * value ]

-- Block with multiple parameters
[ :a :b | a + b ]

-- Stored in variable
doubler := [ :n | n * 2 ].
doubler value: 5  @result{} 10
@end example

@subsubheading Block Evaluation

@example
@var{block} value
@var{block} value: @var{arg1}
@var{block} value: @var{arg1} value: @var{arg2}
@var{block} valueWithArguments: @var{array}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
square := [ :n | n * n ].
square value: 4                @result{} 16

adder := [ :a :b | a + b ].
adder value: 3 value: 5       @result{} 8

-- Delayed execution
delayed := [ Transcript show: 'Later' ].
" ... later ... "
delayed value.
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Parameters: zero or more, each prefixed with colon
@item Body: any sequence of expressions
@item Return: ^ returns from enclosing method, not block
@end itemize

@node Arrays and collections
@section Arrays and Collections

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Arrays and collections store ordered sequences of objects with various
access patterns and iteration methods.

@subsubheading Array Syntax

@example
Array new: @var{size}
@{ @var{item1} . @var{item2} . ... @}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Create array
arr := Array new: 10.

-- Array literal
nums := @{ 1 . 2 . 3 . 4 . 5 @}.
names := @{ 'Alice' . 'Bob' . 'Carol' @}.

-- Nested array
matrix := @{
    @{ 1 . 0 . 0 @} .
    @{ 0 . 1 . 0 @} .
    @{ 0 . 0 . 1 @}
@}.
@end example

@subsubheading Accessing Elements

@example
@var{array} at: @var{index}
@var{array} at: @var{index} put: @var{value}
@end example

Note: Arrays are 1-indexed in standard Smalltalk.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
arr := @{ 10 . 20 . 30 @}.
arr at: 1              @result{} 10
arr at: 2 put: 25.
arr at: 2              @result{} 25
@end example

@subsubheading OrderedCollection

@example
collection := OrderedCollection new.
collection add: item.
collection addAll: otherCollection.
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
items := OrderedCollection new.
items add: 'first'.
items add: 'second'.
items addLast: 'last'.
items do: [ :each | Transcript show: each; cr ].
@end example

@subsubheading Collection Iteration

@example
@var{collection} do: @var{block}
@var{collection} collect: @var{block}
@var{collection} select: @var{block}
@var{collection} reject: @var{block}
@var{collection} detect: @var{block}
@var{collection} inject: @var{initial} into: @var{block}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Iterate
@{ 1 . 2 . 3 . 4 . 5 @} do: [ :n |
    Transcript show: n asString; cr ].

-- Transform
@{ 1 . 2 . 3 @} collect: [ :n | n * n ]  @result{} @{ 1 . 4 . 9 @}

-- Filter
@{ 1 . 2 . 3 . 4 . 5 . 6 @} select: [ :n | n odd ]  @result{} @{ 1 . 3 . 5 @}

-- Find
@{ 3 . 7 . 2 . 8 . 4 @} detect: [ :n | n > 5 ]  @result{} 7
@end example

@node Strings and symbols
@section Strings and Symbols

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Strings represent sequences of characters; symbols are interned
identifiers that are guaranteed unique and can be used as keys.

@subsubheading String Syntax

@example
'@var{text}'
'@var{text with ''escaped'' quotes}'
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
greeting := 'Hello, World!'.
empty := ''.
withQuote := 'It''s a fine day'.
multiLine := 'First line' , 'Second line'.
@end example

@subsubheading String Concatenation

@example
@var{string1} , @var{string2}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
'Hello, ' , 'World!'           @result{} 'Hello, World!'
'Value: ' , 42 asString        @result{} 'Value: 42'
first , ' ' , last             @result{} 'First Last'
@end example

@subsubheading Symbol Syntax

@example
#@var{name}
#'@var{name with spaces}'
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
action := #start.
key := #playerName.
spaces := #'name with spaces'.
@end example

@subsubheading String Operations

@example
@var{string} size
@var{string} at: @var{index}
@var{string} copyFrom: @var{start} to: @var{end}
@var{string} asSymbol
@var{symbol} asString
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
'hello' size                      @result{} 5
'hello' at: 1                     @result{} $h
'hello' copyFrom: 1 to: 3         @result{} 'hel'
'hello' asSymbol                  @result{} #hello
@end example

@node Numbers and arithmetic
@section Numbers and Arithmetic

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Smalltalk provides multiple numeric classes including Integer, Float,
and Fraction for different numeric representations.

@subsubheading Number Formats

@example
@var{integer}          -- decimal: 42
16r@var{hex}           -- hex: 16rFF = 255
8r@var{octal}          -- octal: 8r77 = 63
2r@var{binary}         -- binary: 2r1010 = 10
@var{float}            -- floating point: 3.14
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
42
16rFF                  @result{} 255
8r77                   @result{} 63
2r1010                 @result{} 10
3.14159
-273
@end example

@subsubheading Arithmetic Operators

@example
@var{a} + @var{b}      -- addition
@var{a} - @var{b}      -- subtraction
@var{a} * @var{b}      -- multiplication
@var{a} / @var{b}      -- division (returns Fraction)
@var{a} // @var{b}     -- integer division
@var{a} \\ @var{b}     -- modulo
@var{a} negated        -- unary negation
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
10 + 5                 @result{} 15
10 - 3                 @result{} 7
4 * 3                  @result{} 12
10 / 4                 @result{} (5/2)
10 // 4                @result{} 2
10 \\ 4                @result{} 2
(-5) abs               @result{} 5
@end example

@subsubheading Comparison Operators

@example
@var{a} = @var{b}      -- equality
@var{a} ~= @var{b}     -- inequality
@var{a} < @var{b}      -- less than
@var{a} > @var{b}      -- greater than
@var{a} <= @var{b}     -- less than or equal
@var{a} >= @var{b}     -- greater than or equal
@end example

@subsubheading Number Methods

@example
@var{n} abs           -- absolute value
@var{n} negated       -- negation
@var{n} reciprocal    -- 1/n
@var{n} squared       -- n * n
@var{n} sqrt          -- square root
@var{n} floor         -- floor
@var{n} ceiling       -- ceiling
@var{n} round         -- round to nearest
@var{n} asFloat       -- convert to Float
@var{n} asInteger     -- convert to Integer
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
(-5) abs              @result{} 5
16 sqrt               @result{} 4.0
2.5 floor             @result{} 2
2.5 ceiling           @result{} 3
2.71828 asInteger     @result{} 2
@end example

@node Message sends
@section Message Sends

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Smalltalk uses message passing for all operations. Messages are sent
to objects, which respond by executing the corresponding method.

@subsubheading Unary Messages

No arguments required.

@example
@var{receiver} @var{message}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
10 negated
3.14 rounded
array size
object class
player initialize
@end example

@subsubheading Binary Messages

One argument with operator syntax.

@example
@var{receiver} @var{operator} @var{argument}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
10 + 5
100 - 30
3 * 4
'Hello' , ' World'
score > 100
@end example

@subsubheading Keyword Messages

Multiple arguments with colon-terminated keywords.

@example
@var{receiver} @var{keyword1}: @var{arg1} @var{keyword2}: @var{arg2}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
array at: 1
array at: 1 put: 100
player moveDX: 5 dy: 10
dictionary at: #key put: value
string copyFrom: 1 to: 10
@end example

@subsubheading Cascaded Messages

@example
@var{receiver}
    @var{message1};
    @var{message2};
    @var{message3}
@end example

Multiple messages to the same receiver.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
Transcript
    show: 'Line 1';
    cr;
    show: 'Line 2';
    cr

array
    at: 1 put: 100;
    at: 2 put: 200;
    at: 3 put: 300
@end example

@subsubheading Method Lookup

Messages are resolved at runtime through the object's class hierarchy,
starting with the receiver's class and proceeding to superclasses.

@subsubheading Self and Super

@example
self @var{message}         -- lookup in same class
super @var{message}        -- lookup in superclass
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
Rectangle>>draw
    self drawOutline.
    self fillInterior.

Object subclass: #SpecialPlayer
    instanceVariableNames: ''

SpecialPlayer>>initialize
    super initialize.
    self setupSpecial.
@end example

@node Conditional execution
@section Conditional Execution

@findex ifTrue:
@findex ifFalse:
@findex ifTrue:ifFalse:

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Conditionals are implemented as messages to Boolean objects. The condition
is evaluated first, then the appropriate block is executed.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
@var{condition} ifTrue: @var{trueBlock}
@var{condition} ifFalse: @var{falseBlock}
@var{condition} ifTrue: @var{trueBlock} ifFalse: @var{falseBlock}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Simple ifTrue
score > 100 ifTrue: [
    Transcript show: 'High score!'; cr
].

-- Simple ifFalse
health <= 0 ifFalse: [
    self updateHealthDisplay
].

-- If-else
health <= 0
    ifTrue: [ gameOver := true ]
    ifFalse: [ health := health - damage ].
@end example

@subsubheading Nested Conditionals

@example
temperature > 30 ifTrue: [
    status := #hot
] ifFalse: [
    temperature > 15 ifTrue: [
        status := #warm
    ] ifFalse: [
        status := #cold
    ]
].
@end example

@subsubheading Boolean Messages

@table @asis
@item @code{ifTrue: block} — Execute block if true
@item @code{ifFalse: block} — Execute block if false
@item @code{ifTrue: trueBlock ifFalse: falseBlock} — Conditional execution
@item @code{ifFalse: falseBlock ifTrue: trueBlock} — Reverse conditional
@end table

@subsubheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Condition: any expression returning Boolean
@item Blocks: must be blocks with no parameters
@end itemize

@subsubheading Outputs

Returns the result of the executed block, or nil if no block matches.

@node Loop constructs
@section Loop Constructs

@findex whileTrue:
@findex whileFalse:
@findex to:do:
@findex timesRepeat:

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Loops repeat block execution based on conditions or counts.

@subsubheading While Loops

@example
@var{condition} whileTrue: @var{block}
@var{condition} whileFalse: @var{block}
@end example

The condition is evaluated first, then the block if appropriate.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Pre-test loop
[i < 10] whileTrue: [
    Transcript show: i asString; cr.
    i := i + 1
].

-- Continue while condition is false
[health <= 0] whileFalse: [
    self updateGame
].
@end example

@subsubheading Numeric Range Loop

@example
@var{start} to: @var{end} do: @var{block}
@var{start} to: @var{end} by: @var{step} do: @var{block}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Count 1 to 10
1 to: 10 do: [ :i |
    Transcript show: i asString; cr
].

-- Count with step
0 to: 100 by: 5 do: [ :n |
    process(n)
].

-- Count down
10 to: 1 by: -1 do: [ :n |
    Transcript show: 'T-minus ' , n asString; cr
].
@end example

@subsubheading Times Repeat

@example
@var{n} timesRepeat: @var{block}
@end example

Executes the block @var{n} times.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
5 timesRepeat: [
    Transcript show: 'Hello'; cr
].

-- With counter
10 timesRepeat: [ :i |
    process(i)
].
@end example

@subsubheading Collection Iteration

@example
@var{collection} do: @var{block}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
@{ 1 . 2 . 3 . 4 . 5 @} do: [ :each |
    Transcript show: each asString; cr
].

items do: [ :item |
    item process
].

names select: [ :name | name size > 5 ].
@end example

@subsubheading Breaking from Loops

Use @code{^} (return) to exit, but this returns from the enclosing method:

@example
[ i < 100 ] whileTrue: [
    i := i + 1.
    (items at: i) = target ifTrue: [
        ^ i  "exit method with index"
    ]
].
^nil  "not found"
@end example

For immediate loop exit without method return, use a flag:

@example
| done |
done := false.
[ i < 100 and: [ done not ] ] whileTrue: [
    i := i + 1.
    (items at: i) = target ifTrue: [
        found := i.
        done := true
    ]
].
@end example

@node Input and output
@section Input and Output

@findex print
@findex display
@findex dialogue:
@findex say:
@findex input
@findex read

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

I/O statements handle displaying output and reading input from the user
or environment.

@subsubheading Print Statement

@example
print @var{expression}
print @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, ...
display @var{expression}
display @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, ...
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
print "Hello, World!".
print score.
print "Score: ", score, " Lives: ", lives.
display "Loading..."
@end example

@subsubheading Dialogue Statement

@example
dialogue '@var{speaker}': '@var{text}'
say '@var{text}'
@end example

Displays a dialogue message or message box.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
dialogue 'Narrator': 'Welcome to the adventure!'.
say 'Press any key to continue'.
dialogue 'System': 'Game saved successfully'.
@end example

@subsubheading Input Statement

@example
input @var{variable}
read @var{variable}
ask @var{variable}: '@var{prompt}'
@end example

Reads input into the specified variable.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
input playerName.
ask name: 'Enter your name: '.
ask age: 'How old are you? '.
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Print expressions: any value (converted to string)
@item Input variables: any valid variable name
@item String concatenation: use @code{,} operator
@end itemize

@node Assignment statements
@section Assignment Statements

@findex :=

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Assignment stores a value into a variable.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
@var{variable} := @var{value}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
score := 0.
lives := 3.
playerName := 'Hero'.
counter := counter + 1.
result := self calculateTotal.
@end example

@subsubheading Cascaded Assignment

@example
@var{var1} := @var{var2} := @var{var3} := @var{value}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
x := y := z := 0.
a := b := c := someExpression.
@end example

@subsubheading Instance Variable Assignment

Within methods, instance variables are assigned directly:

@example
@var{instanceVariable} := @var{value}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
Player>>initialize
    x := 0.
    y := 0.
    health := 100.

Player>>setPositionX: xValue y: yValue
    x := xValue.
    y := yValue.
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Variable names: lowercase, alphanumeric
@item Value: any Smalltalk expression
@item Instance variables: declared in class definition
@end itemize

@node Unsupported features
@section Unsupported Features

The following Smalltalk features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O
FileStream operations, directory manipulation, path management

@item Graphics
Drawing primitives, window management, image handling

@item Advanced collections
Set, Bag, Dictionary, Stack, Queue with full API
Weak collections, linked lists

@item Exception handling
@code{Error}, @code{Exception}, @code{Signal}, @code{Halt}

@item Reflection
@code{perform:}, @code{respondsTo:}, @code{instVarAt:}, method introspection

@item Process/Threading
@code{Processor}, @code{Delay}, semaphores, priority scheduling

@item Persistence
Object serialization, change sets, image save/load

@item IDE/Development tools
Browser, debugger, inspector, workspace

@item Network
Socket operations, HTTP, FTP

@item Foreign function interface
ExternalFunction, loading DLLs/shared libraries

@item Metaclasses
Class methods beyond basic factory methods

@item Multiple inheritance
Traits, mixins, method combinations
@end table

@node Smalltalk
